stateDiagram-v2
    direction LR
    state "issued + current" as current
    state "valid use" as valid_use
    state "duplicate_scan" as duplicate_scan
    state "superseded" as superseded
    state "expired" as expired
    state "inactive / invalid" as inactive
    state "state unavailable" as unavailable

    [*] --> current: generate [eligible membership and Redis available] / sign JWT and store active JTI
    current --> valid_use: validate [JWT valid and Redis JTI matches] / allow scanner flow
    valid_use --> current: scanner succeeds / token remains reusable until replaced or expired
    current --> duplicate_scan: check-in replay [same JTI inside duplicate window] / reject 409
    duplicate_scan --> current: duplicate window expires [token still current] / validation may pass again
    current --> superseded: generate newer token / delete old JTI and replace active pointer
    current --> expired: JWT or Redis TTL expires / reject expired or inactive token
    current --> inactive: Redis JTI missing, wrong subject, malformed JWT, or wrong type / reject
    current --> unavailable: Redis read fails / reject 503
    superseded --> [*]
    expired --> [*]
    inactive --> [*]
    unavailable --> [*]

    note bottom of valid_use
        Tokens are rotating, not single-use. Duplicate protection is applied to
        check-in only; check-out validates the same current token without the replay key.
    end note
    note bottom of superseded
        Supersession is the only revocation-like operation represented in code.
        There is no explicit revoke endpoint or durable token table.
    end note
